[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Introduction to Express.js: Routing, Middleware, and Request Handling"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Express.js is a minimal and flexible Node.js web application framework that provides a robust set of features for web and mobile applications. It simplifies the process of building server-side applications with Node.js by providing tools for routing, middleware integration, and request handling."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting up an Express.js Application"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before diving into routing, middleware, and request handling, let's set up a basic Express.js application. First, ensure you have Node.js and npm (Node Package Manager) installed."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create a new directory for your project: 'mkdir my-express-app'"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Navigate into the directory: 'cd my-express-app'"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Initialize a new Node.js project: 'npm init -y'"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Install Express.js: 'npm install express'"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"// index.js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const port = 3000;

app.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Server listening at http://localhost:${port}`);
});"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Save the above code as 'index.js' and run it using 'node index.js'. You should see the message "Server listening at http://localhost:3000" in your console."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Routing in Express.js"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Routing refers to determining how an application responds to a client request to a particular endpoint, which is a URI (Uniform Resource Identifier) and a specific HTTP request method (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, etc.)."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Hello World!');
});

app.post('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Got a POST request at /users');
});

app.put('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Got a PUT request at /users');
});

app.delete('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Got a DELETE request at /users');
});"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, 'app.get()' defines a route that responds to GET requests to the '/' path. Similarly, 'app.post()', 'app.put()', and 'app.delete()' handle POST, PUT, and DELETE requests to the '/users' path, respectively."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Middleware in Express.js"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Middleware functions are functions that have access to the request object (req), the response object (res), and the next middleware function in the application’s request-response cycle. Middleware functions can perform the following tasks:"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Execute any code."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Make changes to the request and the response objects."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"End the request-response cycle."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Call the next middleware function in the stack."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"const logger = (req, res, next) => {
  console.log(`Request received at: ${new Date().toISOString()}`);
  next(); // Call the next middleware function
};

app.use(logger); // Apply the middleware to all routes"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'logger' is a middleware function that logs the timestamp of each request. 'app.use(logger)' applies this middleware to all routes.  The 'next()' function is crucial; it passes control to the next middleware function in the stack. Without it, the request will be left hanging."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Request Handling in Express.js"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Express.js provides convenient ways to access request parameters, query strings, and request bodies."},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"app.get('/users/:userId', (req, res) => {
  const userId = req.params.userId; // Access route parameters
  res.send(`User ID: ${userId}`);
});

app.get('/search', (req, res) => {
  const query = req.query.q; // Access query parameters
  res.send(`Search query: ${query}`);
});

app.use(express.json()); // Middleware to parse JSON request bodies

app.post('/data', (req, res) => {
  const data = req.body; // Access request body
  console.log('Received data:', data);
  res.json({ message: 'Data received successfully', data: data });
});"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, 'req.params' is used to access route parameters (e.g., '/users/123'). 'req.query' is used to access query parameters (e.g., '/search?q=example'). 'req.body' is used to access the request body, but you need to use the 'express.json()' middleware to parse JSON request bodies.  Without 'express.json()', 'req.body' will be undefined."},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"This introduction covers the basics of Express.js routing, middleware, and request handling. These concepts are fundamental to building robust and scalable web applications with Node.js."}]
